Why Remove Instead of Using a Visited Set?

Both approaches work.

Instead of:

Set<String> visited = new HashSet<>();

we simply do:

wordSet.remove(nextWord);

Once a word has been visited, it no longer needs to remain in the dictionary. This saves memory because the same HashSet serves two purposes:

Dictionary lookup
Visited tracking
Complexity Analysis

Let:

N = number of words in the dictionary
L = length of each word

For each visited word:

We try changing every character position (L positions).
For each position, we try all 26 lowercase letters.

So each word generates at most:

L × 26

possible transformations.

Time Complexity: O(N × L × 26), which simplifies to O(N × L) because 26 is a constant.
Space Complexity: O(N) for the HashSet and BFS queue.
Key Takeaways
Convert wordList to a HashSet for O(1) lookups.
Use BFS because the problem asks for the shortest transformation sequence.
Each BFS level represents one additional transformation.
Generate neighbors by changing one character at a time.
Remove words from the HashSet as soon as they are enqueued to avoid revisiting them.
Return level + 1 when endWord is generated, because it represents the next step in the transformation sequence.
If BFS finishes without finding endWord, return 0.